{"type":"thinking","text":"The area-load routine at CPU $9C22 is confirmed. It:\n- LDA $0750; JSR $9C09; TAY → computes the area type index (from $0750)\n- LDA $0750; AND #$1f; STA $074F → area index within type\n- LDA $9CE0,Y; CLC; ADC $074F; TAY; LDA $9CE4,Y -> $E9; LDA $9D06,Y -> $EA → builds the enemy-data pointer $E9/$EA\n- LDY $074E; LDA $9D28,Y; CLC; ADC $074F; TAY; LDA $9D2C,Y -> $E7; LDA $9D4E,Y -> $E8 → builds the layout-data pointer $E7/$E8\n- Then reads the 2-byte area header via (0xe7),Y and unpacks fields into $0744/$0741/$0710/$0715/$0727/$0742/$0743, then advances the pointer by 2 ($E7/$E8 += 2) so it points at the object records.\n\nThat's the routine: it reads the level's data pointers from tables. Entry $9C22, RTS at $9CB3.\n\nNow, I recall the SMB disassembly calls this `GetAreaDataAddrs` or `LoadAreaData`. Let me record it as `LoadAreaData` entry $9C22.\n\nNote: the task says \"find the routine that loads an area when a level starts (the code that reads the level's data pointers)\". This is it: $9C22 builds $E7/$E8 (layout) and $E9/$EA (enemies) from the pointer tables.\n\nNow I need to:\n1. record_mapping for the constants (notes with rom_origins).\n2. record_mapping for the routine (entry $9C22).\n3. promote_mappings once at the end.\n4. use_hack default.\n\nAlso, the task says \"record its entry (where it begins)\". So id = \"0x9C22\".\n\nAlso \"source 'trace and ledgered write'\" for the notes. Hmm, the source field: \"trace and ledgered write\". Let me use that.\n\nLet me also record the max-speed table and the jump/fall tables.\n\nLet me now think about exactly what to record:\n\n1. mapping_type \"note\", id maybe \"JumpMForceData\", name \"JumpMForceData - jump/rise force table\", rom_origins [{mem prgRom, offset 0x3424, length 7}], notes with evidence: located via trace_writes on $009F during a fall (writer PC $C004/$C013) → disassembled gravity routine at $BFD7; found jump init routine at $B4A0-$B4F9 reading $B424,Y->$0709 (rise force), $B42B,Y->$070A (fall force), $B432,Y->$9F (initial speed), $B439,Y->$0433; Y is the speed index (0-4). Proof: wrote prgRom 0x3424 (JumpMForceData[0] $20->$04), reset, reached 1-1, standing jump apex $00CE 124->103 (higher jump); undo restored $20. source \"trace and ledgered write\".\n\n2. mapping_type \"note\", id \"FallMForceData\", name \"FallMForceData - fall/gravity force table\", rom_origins [0x342B length 7], evidence: same routine; proof: wrote 0x342B $70->$10, reset, standing jump descent took ~50 frames (baseline ~20) with $9F crawling 0→3; undo restored $70.\n\n3. mapping_type \"note\", id \"MaxRightXSpdData\", name \"MaxRightXSpdData - max horizontal speed table (walk 0x18)\", rom_origins [0x3443 length 4 (or 3)]. Hmm, let me record offset 0x3443 length 4? The table entries for Y=0..3: 28 18 10 0C. But MaxRightXSpdData is 3 bytes (28 18 10) and 0x0C is a 4th special entry. Let me record offset 0x3443 length 3, notes explaining $B443-$B445 = 28 18 10 (run/walk/slow) and $B446=0x0C special for $0E==7; walk = 0x18 at prgRom 0x3444. And mention MaxLeftXSpdData at 0x3440 (d8 e8 f0) and FrictionData at 0x3447.\n   Proof: wrote 0x3444 0x18->0x40, reset, held Right in 1-1; $0057/$0700 climbed to 63 (0x3F), far past the normal cap 24; undo restored 0x18.\n\n4. mapping_type \"routine\", id \"0x9C22\", name \"LoadAreaData - build level pointer $E7/$E8 and enemy pointer $E9/$EA from the area tables at level start\", notes with the instructions, evidence from disassembly.\n\nAlso maybe record PlayerYSpdData (initial jump speed) as a note. The task asks for jump and gravity force tables + max walking speed. PlayerYSpdData is the initial jump velocity — relevant to \"how hard Mario jumps\". Let me include it.\n\nLet me also record the horizontal accel routine? Not required. The task's 4th item is the area-load routine. I'll record the routine.\n\nHmm, the task says \"record constants with record_mapping mapping_type note and rom_origins (mem prgRom, offset, length), routines with mapping_type routine and the entry address in hex\". So notes for constants, routine for $9C22.\n\nLet me now write the record_mapping calls. I can do several in one block (independent).\n\nLet me be careful with the notes content (concise but with evidence).\n\nAlso, I should double check the \"jump force\" semantics for the record. Let me describe:\n- JumpMForceData ($B424/$3424): 7 bytes 20 20 1E 28 28 0D 04. Indexed by Y = speed class (0-4 from $0700, plus 5-6 for special). Loaded into $0709 at jump start (the gravity/force applied while rising). Smaller = higher jump.\n- FallMForceData ($B42B/$342B): 7 bytes 70 70 60 90 90 0A 09. Loaded into $070A and swapped into $0709 when falling.\n- PlayerYSpdData ($B432/$3432): 7 bytes FC FC FC FB FB FE FF. Initial vertical speed at jump start (negative = up).\n- fraction ($B439/$3439): 7 bytes 00 00 00 00 00 80 00.\n\nWait, actually let me reconsider which table is \"how hard Mario jumps\". The initial speed PlayerYSpdData gives -4/-5, but the rise is clamped to -4 by $02=4. Hmm, so maybe the jump height is mostly governed by JumpMForceData. Both are relevant. I'll record JumpMForceData as \"jump/rise force\" and PlayerYSpdData as \"initial jump speed\".\n\nHmm, let me double check that $B432 is indeed read as the initial speed (yes: `b9 32 b4 / lda $b432,y / 85 9f / sta $9f`). And $B439 -> $0433 (fraction). So initial Y speed = $B432[Y] with fraction $B439[Y].\n\nOK.\n\nNow, one more consideration: the task says \"record constants with ... rom_origins (mem prgRom, offset, length)\". Good.\n\nLet me also make sure the offsets are correct: \n- JumpMForceData at CPU $B424 -> prgRom 0x3424 (13348). ✓ (verified by read)\n- FallMForceData at CPU $B42B -> prgRom 0x342B (13355). ✓\n- PlayerYSpdData at CPU $B432 -> prgRom 0x3432 (13362). ✓\n- fraction at CPU $B439 -> prgRom 0x3439 (13369).\n- MaxRightXSpdData at CPU $B443 -> prgRom 0x3443 (13379). Walk entry $B444 -> 0x3444 (13380). ✓ (verified by write proof)\n- MaxLeftXSpdData at CPU $B440 -> prgRom 0x3440 (13376).\n- FrictionData at CPU $B447 -> prgRom 0x3447 (13383).\n\nLet me record.\n\nActually, wait: I should double-check the max-speed proof corresponds to the right byte. I wrote 0x3444 (CPU $B444) and the speed exceeded 24. If the walk index were Y=1, then $B443+1 = $B444. ✓. Good.\n\nHmm, but actually let me reconsider: is $B444 the \"walk\" entry or is it the \"run\" entry? The table at $B443 = 28 18 10 0C. If Y=0 is running (B held) -> 0x28=40=2.5px/f, Y=1 walking -> 0x18=24=1.5px/f, Y=2 slow (carrying?) -> 0x10=16=1px/f. That matches SMB. And the mapping recorded $0057 max walk = 0x18. And I changed $B444 from 0x18 to 0x40; with normal walking (no B), the speed cap became 0x40. ✓. Great, so $B444 is the walk cap.\n\nNow let me write the record_mapping calls. I'll do 5 notes/routines in one block.\n\nLet me draft each.\n\nNote 1: JumpMForceData\n- mapping_type: \"note\"\n- id: \"JumpMForceData\"\n- name: \"JumpMForceData - jump/rise force table (prgRom 0x3424)\"\n- rom_origins: [{mem:\"prgRom\", offset:0x3424, length:7, notes:\"CPU $B424, 7 bytes 20 20 1E 28 28 0D 04\"}]\n- source: \"trace and ledgered write\"\n- notes: \"Found via trace_writes on Mario's vertical speed $009F: while falling, the only writers were PC $C004/$C013 (prgRom 0x4004/0x4013), which led to the gravity routine $BFD7. The jump-init routine at CPU $B4A0-$B4F9 then showed the tables: $B424,Y -> $0709 (force applied while rising, used by $BF4D/$BF5 9), $B42B,Y -> $070A (fall force), $B432,Y -> $9F (initial jump speed), $B439,Y -> $0433 (fractional speed). Y is a speed class 0-4 from $0700 at the moment of the jump (a standing jump uses Y=0, a full run Y=4). PROOF (ledgered write): write_memory prgRom 0x3424 = $04 (was $20), reset_emulator, reached 1-1, held A 14 frames while watching $00CE: the standing-jump apex moved from $00CE=124 to 103 (rise 52 -> 73 px, a higher/floatier jump); the watch also showed $9F staying at -4 longer. undo_edit restored $20.\"\n\nHmm, careful with prose: \"9\" typo. Let me write cleanly.\n\nNote 2: FallMForceData\n- id: \"FallMForceData\"\n- name: \"FallMForceData - fall/gravity force table (prgRom 0x342B)\"\n- rom_origins: [{mem:\"prgRom\", offset:0x342B, length:7, notes:\"CPU $B42B, 7 bytes 70 70 60 90 90 0A 09\"}]\n- notes: same discovery; proof write 0x342B $70->$10, res","truncated":true}
